<h1 class="title">
CONSTITUTION<br> PRAECONES LEONIS CAMPANAEQUE SOLIS
</h1>

<p class="subtitle">
</p>

<hr class="rule">

<div class="divider">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">I — Purpose of the Order</h3>

<p class="about-text">
1. The Order of the Lion’s Bell exists to investigate, refine, and transmit information. <br>
2. To preserve and transmit knowledge between generations. <br>
3. To serve as a collective of individuals dedicated to wisdom and discovery.<br>
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Information is examined, challenged, and refined,
so that it may be carried forward with clarity and integrity.
The Order commits to the continuous pursuit and transmission of understanding.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
It functions as a structured fellowship of individuals dedicated to the disciplined pursuit of understanding across multiple domains of knowledge, to be carried and preserved in service to others.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The order does not concern itself with temporary politics or worldly disputes.
</p>


<div class="divider">✶ ✶</div>


<h3>II — Core Virtues</h3>

<p>
<strong>Humility</strong><br>
<strong>Perseverance</strong><br>
<strong>Sharing</strong><br>
<strong>Gratitude</strong>
</p>

<p>
Heralds of the Lion's Bell swear to remain humble sages.  
These four virtues form the pillars of the Herald.
</p>

<p>
Heralds of the Lion swear to remain humble sages.<br>
These four virtues form the pillars of the Herald.<br>
For this reason, being a Herald must never be treated as an honor, although
it is of the highest degree.
</p>


<div class="divider">✶ ✶</div>


<h3>III — Structure of the Order</h3>

<p><strong>Outer Circle</strong></p>

<ul class="rank-list">
<li>Seekers</li>
<li>Akousmatikoi</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Inner Circle</strong></p>

<ul class="rank-list">
<li>Heralds of the Lion</li>
<li>Keepers of the Bell</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Inner Sanctum</strong></p>

<ul class="rank-list">
<li>Supreme Herald</li>
<li>The Lion</li>
</ul>


<br>

<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Seekers</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
Seekers stand at the threshold of the Order. 
They are individuals who have shown interest in the Order and are beginning the Lion’s journey, but are not yet initiated.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
A Seeker must show curiosity, sincerity, and interest in knowledge, along with a willingness to question oneself.
A Seeker does not have any formal obligation. The state of Seeker is observational and informal. No oath is required.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Their duties are the following: <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To study the visible and public teachings of the Order <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To question their assumptions and beliefs <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To demonstrate sincerity of intent, intellectual curiosity, and willingness to change <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To prepare themselves for the test leading to Akousmatikoi
</p>

<p class="about-text">
A Seeker has no authority inside the order and no internal participation whatsoever.
</p>

<br>




<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Akousmatikoi</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The Akousmatikoi are initiates of discipline and the first members of the Order.
This rank marks the true entry into the Order. They have begun their journey and started to listen,
just like the great Pythagoras once taught them.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
They are entrusted with the teachings of the Order and are expected to study, 
reflect, and prepare themselves for deeper responsibility.
</p>


<p class="about-text">
An Akousmatikoi must be accepted by at least one Herald and must complete
a period of observation and study, during which they must demonstrate: <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
Clear intention to pursue the path <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
Engagement in discussion <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
Evidence of thought, such as notes, reflections, and questions <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
Acceptance of the principles of the Order
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Akousmatikoi may discuss, but may not formally teach. They are allowed access to internal teachings 
of the Order and participate in discussions, but they cannot represent the Order publicly.
</p>


<br>





<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Heralds</strong></span></h4>


<p class="about-text">
Full members of the Order. 
They have committed themselves to study and now live in duty to share 
and contribute to humanity.
</p>


<p class="about-text">
No longer merely a receiver of knowledge, 
the Herald must be able to articulate it, defend it, live by it, 
and contribute to its growth.
</p>


<p class="about-text">
Advancement to Herald is reviewed by at least
 two Heralds and confirmed by one Keeper of the Bell.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
A disciple must remain in the state of Akousmatikoi for a minimum period of six months of sustained effort.
 During this time, they must demonstrate: <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
consistency of study <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
intellectual development <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
discipline of conduct <br>
<span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
ability to guide others
</p>

<p class="about-text">
They must also contribute in at least two of the following forms: <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span> 
written work, including essays, research, or synthesis <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
teaching, especially the guidance of newer members <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
research and discovery <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
exploration of new ideas <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
connecting disciplines <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
bringing knowledge into the Order
</p>

<p class="about-text">
As fully fledged members of the Order, Heralds have the right to participate
 in decisions and to represent the Order. 
For that reason the Herald must contribute to the collective understanding of the Order, 
share knowledge responsibly and truthfully, 
and embody the principles of the Order in action.
A Herald must think clearly and independently, study without blind obedience, 
and live in accordance with the rules and principles of the Order.
</p>


<br>





<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Keepers of the Bell</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
Senior members entrusted with safeguarding the traditions and teachings of the Order and protecting the integrity of knowledge and the institution.
</p>


<p class="about-text">
A Keeper of the Bell must have served as a Herald for a
 minimum of two years and must have demonstrated stability of character, 
 maturity of judgment, and meaningful contribution to the Order.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Appointment as Keeper of the Bell is decided collectively by the 
 existing Keepers of the Bell and ratified by the Supreme Herald.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Their duties are the following: <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To safeguard the teachings and principles of the Order <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To evaluate knowledge and prevent the corruption of ideas <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To guide Heralds and initiates <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To maintain direction and coherence within the Order <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To oversee the accuracy and depth of teaching <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To resolve internal disputes <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To decide matters concerning structure and integrity
</p>

<p class="about-text">
    A Keeper must be free from ego-driven authority, must place truth above personal belief, 
and must act as a stabilizing force within the fellowship.
</p>



<br>


<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Supreme Herald</strong></span></h4>




<p class="about-text">
    The Supreme Herald is chosen collectively by the Keepers of the Bell.
    The Supreme Herald is the final coordinating authority beneath the Lion
</p>

<p class="about-text">
    The Supreme Herald serves as the axis of the Order’s 
structural and intellectual direction.

</p>

<p class="about-text">
    Their duties are the following: <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To coordinate the Order <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To ensure alignment with its founding principles <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To oversee major decisions and developments <br>
 <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
To maintain unity among all levels of the Order <br>
</p>

<p class="about-text">
    Their duties are to maintain unity and
 coherence and to prevent fragmentation within the Order. 
The Supreme Herald must possess the ability to unify without
 domination and must be free of personal agenda.
</p>


<br>





<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>The Lion</strong></span></h4>


<p class="about-text">
    The Lion serves as the singular 
foundation and highest authority of the Order. 
</p>

<p class="about-text">
    He stands as the point of reference for the entire
 structure and embodies the highest ideal of the Herald.
</p>


<p class="about-text">
 The office of the Lion is not a rank to be attained through advancement.
  It is a singular position, inseparably tied to the origin and identity of the Order.
  There cannot be another Lion, as he is the reincarnation of the great Sage.
</p>


<p class="about-text">In the founder’s absence, the Supreme Herald and the Keepers of the Bell serve as
the guiding authority of the Order.</p>


<p class="about-text">
  The Lion holds final authority and oversight in all matters concerning the
   Order and retains the power of veto over all structural and doctrinal decisions.
</p>






<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>

<h3 class="about-subtitle">IV — On Voting</h3>

<p class="about-text">
Heralds of the Lion (including the Supreme Herald) and Keepers of the Bell 
 may vote on general matters of the Order.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The Keepers of the Bell, together with the Supreme Herald, 
hold decisive weight in structural and doctrinal matters.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The Council of Heralds is composed of Heralds of the Lion, Keepers of the Bell, the Supreme Herald, and the Lion (if present).
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The Lion holds final authority above all ranks and retains the power of veto.
</p>







<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">V — On Initiation</h3>


<p class="about-text">
Only Heralds and those above them may initiate Akousmatikoi.
Such initiation must be witnessed or approved by another Herald or higher-ranking member of the Order.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
    Advancement to the rank of
Herald requires the approval of at least two Heralds and confirmation by a Keeper of the Bell.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The Supreme Herald is chosen by a majority of the Council of Heralds.
</p>







<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">VI — On the Trials of Advancement</h3>

<p class="about-text">
Advancement within the Order is not granted lightly, but must be proven 
through trials of discipline, understanding, conduct, and contribution.
</p>

<br>
<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Advancement: Akousmatikoi to Herald</strong></span></h4>



<p class="about-text">
    1. The candidate must present an idea, concept, or argument in a clear and structured form.
</p>


<p class="about-text">
    This trial exists to demonstrate clarity of thought, 
intellectual independence, and the ability to articulate and defend knowledge.
</p>

<br>

<p class="about-text">
    2. The candidate must demonstrate discipline through sustained observance
 of the rules of conduct throughout the entire Akousmatikoi period.
</p>


<p class="about-text">
     This trial exists to show consistency, improvement, and resistance to stagnation.
</p>

<br>

<p class="about-text">
     3. The candidate must show that
 they are living in accordance with the founding principles of the Order.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
     This trial measures integrity in real situations and the ability to act
  according to principle rather than merely speak of it.
</p>



<br>
<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Advancement: Herald to Keeper</strong></span></h4>


<p class="about-text">
     1. The Herald must demonstrate the ability to 
  add real value to the Order through significant contribution.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
  Such contribution may take the form of teaching,
   writing, mentoring, research, or the structuring of knowledge. 
       This trial exists to demonstrate depth of understanding
 and usefulness to the Order and its members.
</p>


<br>

<p class="about-text">
     2. The Herald must prove the ability to discern truth from 
 falsehood with clarity and fairness.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
    This includes demonstration of interdisciplinary
 breadth of knowledge, resistance to bias, freedom from ego,
  and the capacity for sound judgment.
</p>



<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">VII - On the Authority and Auxiliary Texts</h3>

<p class="about-text">
The Constitution of the Order of the Lion’s Bell stands as the highest formal structure of the Order.
From it proceed the Rules of Conduct, doctrines, and all auxiliary texts that define the life, discipline, and teachings of the Herald.
These texts are binding insofar as they remain in alignment with the Constitution and the founding principles of the Order.
The Rules of Conduct serve as the practical expression of the Constitution and are to be observed by all Heralds.
Additional writings, teachings, and commentaries may be recognized and preserved by the Keepers of the Bell as part of the living body of knowledge of the Order.
No auxiliary text may contradict the Constitution. In the event of conflict, the Constitution prevails.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">VIII - On the Number of Heralds</h3>

<p class="about-text">
The number of Heralds within the Order shall not exceed 432.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Seekers and Akousmatikoi are not counted within this number.
Number also excludes all that carry the title of the Herald: Keepers of the Bell, the Supreme Herald and the Lion.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
This limitation exists to preserve clarity of structure, integrity of knowledge, and strengh and familiarity within the Order.
No new Herald may be admitted beyond this number unless a place becomes vacant.
</p>

<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>



<h3 class="about-subtitle">IX - On the Number of Keepers of the Bell</h3>

<p class="about-text">
The number of Keepers of the Bell shall not exceed six.
This limitation exists to preserve clarity of judgment, unity of direction, and the integrity of decision-making within the Order.
</p>




<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">X - On Vacant Positions</h3>


<p class="about-text">
When a Herald becomes inactive or is removed, their place within the Order is considered vacant.
Only upon such vacancy may a new Herald be admitted, in accordance with the established rules of advancement.
</p>




<div class="divider-symbol">✶✶</div>



<h3 class="about-subtitle">XI - On Inactive Heralds</h3>

<p class="about-text">
A Herald who ceases to participate in the life of the Order for an extended period may be designated as inactive.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
An inactive Herald retains the title in name, but relinquishes: <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
participation in decisions <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
authority within the Order <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
active responsibilities <br>
</p>

<p class="about-text">
A Herald may return to active status upon review and approval by the Keepers of the Bell.
</p>




<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">XII - On the Death of a Herald</h3>


<p class="about-text">
Upon the death of a Herald, their place within the Order becomes vacant.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Their name, works, and contributions are to be preserved as part of the shared memory of the Order.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">XIII - On False Representation</h3>

<p class="about-text">
Any member who falsely claims rank, authority, or position within the Order shall be subject to removal.
Deliberate misrepresentation of one’s status constitutes a violation of the integrity of the Order and shall result in the stripping of all titles and positions.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">XIV - On the Process of Removal</h3>


<p class="about-text">
A complaint concerning violation of conduct or principles may be submitted by any Herald.
Such a complaint shall be reviewed by the Keepers of the Bell.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The matter shall be examined with fairness, clarity, and without prejudice.
A decision of removal requires a majority agreement of the Keepers of the Bell.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">XV - On the Authority of the Lion and Absence</h3>

<p class="about-text">The Lion stands as the symbolic and foundational figure of the Order, representing its origin, direction, and highest ideal.
The Lion does not stand above the principles of the Order, but is bound to them as their first bearer.
The role of the Lion is to preserve coherence, provide direction, and safeguard the integrity of the Order.
While the Lion retains final oversight in matters concerning the Order, this authority exists in service to the principles and not above them.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
In the absence of the Lion, the authority of the Order shall be maintained by two Supreme Heralds, chosen from among the Keepers of the Bell.
These two shall act jointly to preserve balance, continuity, and stability within the Order.
No single individual shall assume the full authority of the Lion.
</p>

<p class="about-text">  In the year 34500 since Zep Tepi, <br>
    The Lion of the Order is <span class="accent">Lekistra.</span>
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">XVI - On Mentorship</h3>

<p class="about-text">
A Herald who accepts a disciple assumes responsibility for their guidance.
This responsibility is not one of authority or obedience.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The Herald must: <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
guide the disciple in study and conduct <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
correct error where it appears <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
encourage independent thought
</p>


<p class="about-text">
The mentorship between Herald and disciple is not permanent. <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
It concludes when: <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
the disciple advances to the rank of Herald <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
the initiation is withdrawn <br>
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Upon advancement, the former disciple stands as an equal within the Order.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>

<h3 class="about-subtitle">XVII - On the Withdrawal of Initiation</h3>

<p class="about-text">
A Herald retains the authority to withdraw their disciple from the path prior to full initiation.
This may be enacted if the disciple: <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
fails to demonstrate discipline <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
violates the principles of the Order <br>
     <span class="rank-symbol">*</span>
or shows lack of sincerity in purpose
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Such withdrawal constitutes recognition that the individual is not yet ready for advancement.
</p>

<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">XVIII - On Re-Entry After Withdrawal</h3>

<p class="about-text">
A candidate whose initiation has been withdrawn must wait a period of three years before seeking entry into the Order again.
This period exists to allow for reflection, growth, and genuine change of character.
Re-entry is not guaranteed and must be evaluated as a new candidacy.
</p>


<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>


<h3 class="about-subtitle">XIX  -   On the Name of the Herald</h3>


<p class="about-text">
Upon becoming a Herald, a member may choose a name by which they are known within the Order.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Such a name must be approved by the Supreme Herald.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
Once sworn under this name, it shall not be changed.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The chosen name represents the identity of the Herald within the Order and stands as part of their commitment.
<p>



<div class="divider-symbol">✶ ✶</div>




    <h3 class="about-subtitle">XX  -   Disciplines of the Order</h3>


<p class="about-text">
The Order of the Lion’s Bell concerns itself with the investigation of knowledge across multiple domains of human understanding.
The Order recognizes seven primary disciplines through which knowledge is investigated and refined.
These disciplines, known as the Septem Artes Praeconis, form the intellectual foundation of the Herald.
<p>

<p class="about-text">
They are: <br>
1. Philosophia <br>
2. Scientia <br>
3. Traditio <br>
4. Conscientia <br>
5. Natura Informationis <br>
6. Phenomena Incognita <br>
7. Cultura et Ars <br>
</p> 

<br>

<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Philosophy</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The study of reason, truth, ethics, and the nature of existence.
This domain forms the foundation of clear thinking and intellectual discipline within the Order.
</p>

<br>


<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Science</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The investigation of the natural world through observation, experimentation, and analysis.
The Order values scientific rigor as a method of refining and validating understanding.
</p>

<br>

<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Tradition</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The study of past civilizations, events, belief systems, and inherited knowledge.
This includes established historical understanding and the investigation of lost or incomplete records of human development.
</p>

<br>

<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Consciousness</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The study of perception, awareness, and subjective experience.
This domain seeks to understand the nature of mind and the conditions of human experience.
</p>


 <br>

<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Information</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The study of the nature, structure, transmission, and interpretation of information.
</p>


<br>

<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Anomalous Phenomena</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The investigation of phenomena not yet fully explained by current scientific frameworks.
</p>


<br>

<h4 class="about-text"><span class="accent"><strong>Culture and Expression</strong></span></h4>

<p class="about-text">
The Herald as a participant in culture and creator of expression.
This domain is concerned with art, language, symbolism and sharing of ideas.
</p>

<p class="about-text">
The Order does not claim certainty in all matters,
